            <ArticleTitle>The effect of intraperitoneal ropivacaine on pain after laparoscopic excision of endometriosis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>29-34</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To compare the effects of ropivacaine and saline on pain after laparoscopic excision of endometriosis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Randomized, double-blind, controlled trial (Canadian Task Force classification I).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Women's hospital.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Ninety-three women undergoing excision of endometriosis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">Laparoscopic excision of endometriosis, after which saline 100 mg (46 women) or ropivacaine 200 mg dissolved in 100 ml saline (47) was instilled intraperitoneally for postoperative analgesia.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MEASUREMENTS AND MAIN RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">No differences were seen between groups during the first 7 hours after surgery with respect to pain scores, analgesic requirements, postoperative nausea and vomiting, or time to discharge.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">We found no difference between ropivacaine and saline, instilled intraperitoneally, in their effects on pain after extensive excision of endometriosis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
